# SESSION 2: Understanding Git Architecture and Working Mechanism
Objective: Students should understand how Git works internally and how it manages project versions.

# PART 1 – Understanding Git Architecture

## 1. What happens when we execute `git init`?
Suppose we are developing a bakery website.
Our project structure is:

the-fairy-bake/
│
├── index.html
├── about.html
├── contact.html
├── style.css
└── script.js

Initially, these are just normal files and folders.

Now execute:
git init

What happens?

Git creates a hidden directory named: 
.git
Our project becomes:

the-fairy-bake/
│
├── .git/
├── index.html
├── about.html
├── contact.html
├── style.css
└── script.js

The `.git` directory is the internal database of the Git repository.
It maintains the information required for version control, including:

- Commit history.
- Branch information.
- Repository configuration.
- File snapshots.
- References.
- Git objects.

Let's inspect it.

ls -la .git

On Windows PowerShell:

Get-ChildItem -Force .git

Typical Git directory structure:

.git/
│
├── HEAD
├── config
├── description
├── hooks/
├── info/
├── objects/
├── refs/
└── index

Let's understand each component.

| Component | Purpose |
| HEAD | Identifies the currently checked-out branch or commit |
| config | Repository-specific configuration |
| objects | Stores Git objects |
| refs | Stores references to branches and tags |
| hooks | Contains scripts that can run during Git events |
| index | Maintains staging-area information |
| info | Contains additional repository information |

Important classroom question:
What happens if we delete the `.git` directory?
Answer: We remove the repository's local Git metadata and history. The project files generally remain, but Git no longer recognizes that directory as the same repository. This is an excellent way to explain the difference between a normal project folder and a Git repository.

# PART 2 – Git's Three Important Areas

This is arguably the most important concept in the entire Git course.
Git uses three important areas:
### 1. Working Directory
The working directory is the location where developers create, modify and delete project files.

For example:
project/
    index.html
    style.css
    app.js
Suppose you open `index.html` and change:
<h1>Welcome</h1>

To:
<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake</h1>

You have modified the file.
However, Git has not yet recorded this modification in a commit.

### 2. Staging Area
The staging area is where developers select the changes they want to include in their next commit.

Command:
git add index.html

Now the changes are staged.

Explain it using a real-life analogy:

Imagine you are packing a school bag.

- Working directory = Your entire room.
- Staging area = The items you select to put in your bag.
- Local repository = The completed record of what you have packed.

You do not necessarily select everything in your room. Similarly, Git allows you to select specific changes for a commit.

### 3. Local Repository
The local repository stores committed snapshots of your project.

Command:
git commit -m "Updated homepage"

Now Git records a new commit.

### Complete Git workflow

       WORKING DIRECTORY
       (Modify files)
              |
              |
           git add
              |
              v
         STAGING AREA
       (Select changes)
              |
              |
          git commit
              |
              v
       LOCAL REPOSITORY
       (Commit history)

Remember these three commands:

| Command | Purpose |
| `git status` | Checks the current state |
| `git add` | Stages changes |
| `git commit` | Records staged changes |

# PART 3 – Understanding the Git File Lifecycle

Now introduce another important concept: Git File Lifecycle.

A file can be in different states.

There are four important states.

### State 1: Untracked

A file exists in the project directory, but Git is not tracking it.

Example:

touch index.html
Check:
git status

WE may see:

Untracked files:
    index.html
Meaning: Git knows that this file exists, but it has not been added to the staging area.

### State 2: Unmodified
A file has been committed and has not been changed since its last commit.

Example:

git add index.html
git commit -m "Added homepage"

Now the file is committed and clean.

### State 3: Modified

Suppose we change the HTML file.

<h1>Welcome to Git Training</h1>

Check:
git status

Git reports that the file has been modified.

Meaning: The current working-directory version differs from the committed version.

### State 4: Staged

Now execute:
git add index.html

The modification moves into the staging area.

Git is now ready to include that version in the next commit.

Important distinction:

A file can be staged and then modified again.

For example:

1. Modify index.html.
2. Execute `git add index.html`.
3. Modify index.html again.

Now the staged version and working-directory version are different.

The first modification is staged, but the second modification is not.

This is a very useful demonstration for students.

# PART 4 – Live Practical: Understanding the Complete Git Workflow
Now conduct this practical in front of students.

## Practical 02: Create and Manage our First Git Repository

Project: The Fairy Bake

### Step 1. Create a project directory

mkdir the-fairy-bake
cd the-fairy-bake

### Step 2. Initialize Git
git init

### Step 3. Create the first HTML file

On Ubuntu:
nano index.html

On Windows PowerShell:
notepad index.html

Add:
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>The Fairy Bake</title>
</head>
<body>

    <h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake</h1>

    <p>Fresh cakes and bakery products.</p>

</body>
</html>

Save the file.

### Step 4. Check Git status
git status

Expected observation:
Untracked files:
    index.html
    
Explain why Git calls it untracked.

### Step 5. Add the file
git add index.html

Check:
git status

Expected observation:
Changes to be committed:
    new file: index.html
    
### Step 6. Commit the file
git commit -m "Initial bakery website"

The expected output will indicate that a new commit was created.

### Step 7. Verify commit history
git log

For a compact history:
git log --oneline

Example:
a83f921 Initial bakery website

Our commit ID will be different.

Explain what a commit ID is.
Every Git commit has a unique identifier generated using a cryptographic hash (SHA-1 by default, with SHA-256 also supported in newer Git repositories).

This identifier helps Git identify and reference a particular commit.

# PART 5 – Understanding Important Git Commands

Now learn the commands individually.

## 1. git status
git status

Purpose: Shows the current state of the working directory and staging area.
Use it whenever we are unsure about what Git is tracking.

## 2. git add
git add index.html

Stages a particular file.
To stage all changes in the current directory:

git add .

To stage all tracked modifications and deletions, along with untracked files:

git add -A

Difference:

| Command | Meaning |
| `git add index.html` | Stage one file |
| `git add .` | Stage changes under the current directory |
| `git add -A` | Stage all changes in the working tree |

## 3. git commit
git commit -m "Added homepage"

Creates a commit containing staged changes.

Explain commit message best practices.

Good examples:
Added login functionality
Updated homepage design
Fixed navigation menu
Added database connection

Bad examples:
Changes
Update
Hello
Final
Testing

## 4. git log
git log

Shows commit history.

Useful variations:

git log --oneline
git log --graph
git log --all --oneline --decorate --graph

The last command is particularly useful when students start learning branches.

## 5. git diff

This command is extremely important.

Suppose we modify `index.html`:

<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake</h1>
<p>Fresh cakes and bakery products.</p>

Change it to:

<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake Bakery</h1>
<p>Fresh cakes, cookies and bakery products.</p>

Now execute:
git diff

Git displays the differences between the working directory and the staging area.

You may see:

-<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake</h1>
+<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake Bakery</h1>

Explain:

- `-` represents removed content.
- `+` represents added content.

Other useful variations:
git diff --staged
git diff HEAD

| Command | Purpose |
| `git diff` | Working directory vs staging area |
| `git diff --staged` | Staging area vs last commit |
| `git diff HEAD` | Working directory and staging area combined vs last commit |

# PART 6 – Understanding Git Snapshots

Many beginners think Git stores complete copies of files every time they commit.

Git is better understood as a snapshot-based version control system.

For example:

Commit 1

index.html
style.css

Commit 2

index.html (modified)
style.css

Commit 3

index.html (modified)
style.css (modified)
script.js (new)

Each commit represents the state of the tracked project at that point in time.

Internally, Git uses objects to represent and connect this information.

There are four important Git object types:

| Object | Purpose |
| Blob | Stores file content |
| Tree | Represents directory structure and references files/subdirectories |
| Commit | Records a snapshot and metadata |
| Tag | Provides a named reference to an object, commonly a commit |
